TSM client.dll -- reTS's client DLL, built from source
======================================================

What this is
------------

ts\cl_dlls\client.dll is built from the reTS client source (cl_dll\), not from the stock TS
client plus a binary patch. The cloak fix that used to be a binary patch is now a source change
in cl_dll\view.cpp; its byte-level history is kept as an appendix, together with the stock
client it was applied to.

Deployed file : client.dll
Built by      : reTS\scripts\build-win32-dropin.ps1 with RETS_REBUILD=1
                (CMake, Win32, Release; target client)
Size          : 838656 bytes
SHA256        : B2349B36E0E3F86247CBB50D74F2127F3440FFFE5DEF3D65FA8BE7F8B42ED6B3
Source base   : goldsrc-net/the-specialists main through bd3f6c3c1aa3 (2026-10-29),
                plus the local mouse-release fix below, the 128-slot build (2026-11-01) and the
                scoreboard's pictures, link icons, lives column, scrolling, Roleplay
                heading, level and profile double-click, with hud_classautokill out of the
                userinfo (2026-11-02), and the level badges, the server panels (H9
                VGUI), the score and kills columns and the PPSh-41 (2026-11-03)

What the source build contains, beyond the stock client
-------------------------------------------------------

1. Local-player cloak alpha -- now in source

   The stock client has a per-frame block (around RVA 0x4d0af) that overwrites the local
   player's curstate rendermode/renderamt with a fixed third-person transparency taken from
   cl_thirdtransparency, so a server cloak (tsm_cloak_opacity) never reached the cloaker's own
   model. The binary patch added a pre-check that skipped that block when the server had already
   sent kRenderTransTexture with renderamt below 255. The same check is now in
   cl_dll\view.cpp, in the same block: bServerCloak skips the third-person fade, so the
   server's state survives to the studio draw. The hard reset (spectator/dead/first-person) is
   unchanged, and every non-cloak frame takes the original path.

   Verified in the built DLL: cmp [edi+0x2f8],2 / cmp [edi+0x2fc],0xff, then skip the fade.

2. Mouse-button releases forwarded while the cursor is up (new-round magdump fix)

   cl_dll\inputw32.cpp, IN_MouseEvent. While the cursor is up (the TS buy menu, a VGUI
   panel, the spectator camera) the function gives the click to the menu and returns
   without turning it into a key event. That is correct for a press, but it also ate the
   RELEASE of a button that was already held when the cursor came up. The TS buy menu
   opens automatically on entering spectate at a round start, so a fire button held
   across that edge (the round-end shot, or a click that closed the menu) kept its
   kbutton down: in_attack stayed set, the client predicted fire through the freeze, and
   at unfreeze the server saw IN_ATTACK still down and emptied the magazine, with the
   client-side aim recoil climbing to the ceiling. The cursor-up branch now forwards a
   Key_Event(K_MOUSE1 + i, 0) for every button that went from down to up, before syncing
   mouse_oldbuttonstate. Presses are still consumed by the menu; a release whose press
   was consumed matches no down key in KeyUp and is dropped by the engine while the key
   is up, so it is harmless.

3. Gunshot and ricochet pitch unclamped (upstream bd3f6c3c1aa3)

   cl_dll\ev_ts.cpp: the 255/1 clamp is gone from the gunshot, linked-fire and ricochet
   pitches. pitch = 100 + 300 * slow, 400 at normal speed; neither GoldSrc nor Xash caps pitch,
   so normal fire plays at 4x again instead of 2.55x.

4. Built for 128 player slots (docs\PLAYER_LIMIT_128.txt)

   MAX_PLAYERS 128 (dlls\cdll_dll.h) and MAX_PLAYERS_HUD = MAX_PLAYERS + 1, so the HUD's player
   lists, the spectator, the overview map, the team menu and the motion-blur ghosts cover every
   slot.
   - Voice: "vban" holds one mask per 32 slots in use ("vban <mask>" on a server of 32 or
     fewer), and VoiceMask is read for as many [rules, ban] pairs as the message carries.
   - The scoreboard (vgui_ScorePanel.cpp) shows up to 64 players on one screen, without
     scrolling: two columns side by side, one per team (team 1 left, team 2 right; free-for-all
     split in two), a team of more than a column continuing in the other one under "(cont.)".
     Each row: an avatar cell (the default silhouette until a picture for the slot arrives),
     status in short form (DEAD, 3K, 6K, SPEC, UNSTOP, ONE, WAIT), name, kills, deaths, assists,
     ping ("BOT" for a player whose userinfo has *bot 1) and the voice icon. Players with no team
     are listed in a strip at the bottom; the title line has the server, the map and the player
     count. Rows are 12 units high (YRES), shorter when a layout needs more rows, and the fonts
     follow the row height.
   - H9Assists (byte slot, short assists; slot 0 says the server counts assists), sent by H9's
     ts_matchmode, fills the assists column. A server that never sends it leaves it empty.
   - The team menu's name list holds every slot's name, and its strncat bounds leave room for
     the terminator.

6. The scoreboard's havoc9.org pictures (HUD_H9Avatar)

   An export, HUD_H9Avatar(int slot, int version, int wide, int tall, const unsigned char *rgba)
   (vgui_ScorePanel.cpp), through which an H9XASH engine with engine-h9-avatars.patch hands over
   a slot's picture: RGBA pixels, at most 64 x 64, which the slot's avatar cell draws scaled to
   the cell in place of the silhouette; rgba NULL puts the silhouette back. The bitmap a new
   picture replaces is freed at the next level, since a cell may still hold it. Nothing else
   calls it: on another engine, or on a server that sends no H9Avatar, every cell keeps the
   silhouette.

7. The scoreboard's link icons (H9Link, cl_h9_linkicons)

   Each row's avatar cell carries a small, half-transparent icon in its lower right corner (the
   whole cell in rows of 12 pixels or less): a green chain for a player the server's h9_presence
   names a proven havoc9.org account (the H9Link user message, byte slot and byte linked, hooked
   in hud.cpp), a red broken chain for anyone else, and H9's helmet in grey for a bot, which
   never gets a chain (H9Link byte 2, which h9_presence sends from is_user_bot; *bot 1 in the
   userinfo on a server without it). The icons are gfx/vgui/h9_link.tga, h9_unlink.tga and
   h9_bot.tga (H9's own drawing, tools/make_link_icons.py), read once; a missing file is no
   icon. cl_h9_linkicons 0 (a new archived client cvar) leaves them out. On a server without
   h9_presence every row is red; on GoldSrc as on H9XASH. vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9 LINK ICONS
   blocks.

8. The scoreboard's lives column (H9Lives)

   A LIVES cell between A and PING: each player's lives left, the one being played included, out
   of his class's lives ("1/2"), "-" on a side that never runs out, and in each team's header row
   the sum of its players' ("9/14"). ts_matchmode sends the H9Lives user message (byte slot, byte
   left, byte lives; left 255 for a side that never runs out, lives 0 for nobody counted; slot 0
   empties the column), hooked in hud.cpp and read into extra_player_info_t's lives_left and
   lives_max. The column stays empty on a server that sends none, as A does without H9Assists.
   vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9 LIVES blocks, SB_CELL_LIVES in vgui_ScorePanel.h.

9. The scoreboard scrolls (cl_h9_scoreboard_scroll)

   A column too long for the board even at its shortest rows keeps its team in its own column (a
   free-for-all list split between the two) and scrolls instead of ending in "... N more": while
   the board is held open the mouse wheel moves the column under the pointer, or both without
   one, three rows a notch, and PgUp/PgDn a page (TeamFortressViewport::KeyInput takes those keys
   only while a column can move, so the wheel changes weapons the rest of the time). The motion
   eases every frame (ScrollThink); row widget 0 of a column, the team header, stays pinned, the
   others sit in a clip panel under it; a thin half-transparent bar at a scrolling column's edge
   shows the place. Closing or opening the board resets it. cl_h9_scoreboard_scroll 0 (an archived
   client cvar, default 1) keeps "... N more". vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9 SCROLL blocks.

10. The scoreboard's heading for a list without teams (H9Board)

   On a map without teams the scoreboard heads its one list "Free for all". H9's ts_matchmode
   sends each joining player the H9Board user message (a string), "Roleplay" on a roleplay map,
   which the heading reads instead; without one, a map named like a roleplay map (rp_ or tsrp_ at
   the start, _rp_ or _tsrp_ inside: ts_matchmode's roleplay_map rule) reads "Roleplay" too.
   vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9 BOARD block, hooked in hud.cpp.

11. hud_classautokill is no longer in the userinfo

   hud.cpp, CHud::Init: hud_classautokill is created FCVAR_ARCHIVE alone, without FCVAR_USERINFO.
   Only the client reads it (the team and class menus, vgui_CustomObjects.cpp); no server code
   does, so it no longer takes room in the player's userinfo, and changing it no longer sends a
   userinfo update. It is still the player's own archived toggle, default 1.

12. The scoreboard's level badge (H9Level)

   h9_presence 1.6 tells every client each proven havoc9.org account's level on the H9 ladder, Lv
   0 to 99 (docs\H9_COMPETITIVE.txt section 8a), in the H9Level user message (byte slot, byte
   level, 255 for none; a joining client gets the whole table, and a slot that empties is
   cleared), hooked in hud.cpp. The scoreboard draws the level's badge before the player's name:
   the picture gfx\vgui\h9_levels\lv-<N>.tga (64 x 64, 32-bit RLE TGA with alpha, the art of
   havoc9.org's own badges, drawn by modelling\make-level-badges.mjs), read once on first use
   with the link icons' TGA reader, area-filtered with its alpha to the avatar's size (the row
   height less 2), and drawn at the left edge of the name cell, centred on the row, with the name
   moved right past it and fitted to what is left. The row's own colour shows around the badge,
   and fully clear pixels carry the badge's edge colour, so a filtered draw never darkens the
   rim. The bitmaps are kept by level and size for the life of the client, as the silhouettes
   are. Where a level's picture is missing the row shows the text "Lv N" before the name
   instead. Bots, players without an account and games that did not prove their player id show
   none, and so does every row on a server that sends no H9Level. vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9
   LEVELS and H9 LEVEL BADGES blocks (GetLevelBitmap, CLevelBitmap, and CLabelHeader's lead
   picture, setLeadImage, in vgui_ScorePanel.h). The pictures ship beside client.dll in
   ts\gfx\vgui\h9_levels\ (export.bat; the Xash overlay and H9XASH carry them too).

13. A double-click on a linked player's picture opens their havoc9.org profile (h9_profile)

   On an engine that has the h9_profile command (H9XASH with engine-h9-avatars.patch; looked up
   once in the engine's command list), a double-click on the picture of a row with a green chain
   (H9Link 1, not a bot), your own row's too, runs h9_profile <slot>: the engine opens that
   account's havoc9.org profile in the web browser, the address built from its own site and the
   account the server named for the slot's picture. A single click on such a picture mutes nobody,
   so a double-click never mutes the player; the rest of the row mutes as before. On an engine
   without h9_profile (Steam's, the browser build) nothing changes. vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9
   PROFILES blocks; mouseDoublePressed and ProfileSlotAt in vgui_ScorePanel.h. The level's name
   cell and the picture's cell are apart, so the two work side by side.

14. Server panels: H9 VGUI (H9Vgui, cl_h9_vgui, _h9vgui)

   A server plugin can draw real VGUI panels on an H9 player's screen, in the textscheme fonts on
   the art of sprites\tsm_panel.spr and sprites\tsm_banner.spr (docs\H9_VGUI.txt). One
   plugin-registered user message, H9Vgui, carries the twin of an AMX text menu (MENU, ROWS,
   TWIN), a notice in place of a hudmessage (NOTICE), and a plugin's own layout of labels,
   built-in images, number-key buttons and progress bars (PANEL, CTRL, SET, SHOW, HIDE, CLOSE,
   COMMIT). A menu's panel shows only while CHudMenu has the AMX menu it stands for -- a TWIN came
   just before it with the same keys and the CRC-16 of the same text -- and CHudMenu then draws no
   text; its number keys still send the same menuselect. Panels never take the mouse or the
   keyboard, keep inside a safe area clear of TS's kill notices (the top 136 px, 104 under 900 px
   of height) and of the chat and HUD (the bottom 196 px), keep off the crosshair, and ESC closes
   the newest. The game says it draws them in the userinfo key _h9vgui: 2, or 0 with cl_h9_vgui
   0, which also refuses them all; cl_h9_vgui_scale and cl_h9_vgui_top tune the size and the top
   band. Every limit is the client's own: 8 panels, 48 controls, 16 rows a menu, 6 notices, 64
   messages a second, 192 bytes a message, ids of a-z 0-9 . _ -, no image but "#panel" and
   "#banner"; a panel's reply goes to the server with pfnServerCmd, "h9vgui <id> <control>
   <value>". h9_vgui.cpp and vgui_H9Panel.cpp (new), hooked in hud.cpp (H9Vgui_Init), menu.cpp
   (the pairing), ammo.cpp (a panel's number keys) and vgui_TeamFortressViewport.cpp (ESC);
   cl_dll\CMakeLists.txt lists the new files.

   Protocol 2 (_h9vgui "2") adds STATUS (op 21): a notice that stays in its slot until another
   replaces it or an empty text clears it, stacks first under TS's notice band, and has its {t}
   (m:ss) and {s} (seconds) counted down by the client itself from the one message. ts_matchmode
   sends its round line, freeze countdown, warmup readout and a round's or a match's result as
   one status, the match bar: two lines on the tsm_banner art centred under the band, the state
   and its clock over the score, sent again only when what it says changes. The first banner at
   the top keeps above 0.25 of the height where the band leaves it 40 px for that, and on a
   phone's screen takes its art's own size. With every notice slot taken, a new notice takes the
   oldest notice's, never a status's. A game of protocol 1 is sent no status and keeps the
   hudmessages. A one-line banner is no taller than its text and the art's corners, and the top
   stack goes on below the crosshair box when the next notice would reach into it. Menus and
   panels on the tsm_panel art draw at 0.68 of the screen height over 480 (0.8 before), times
   cl_h9_vgui_scale. h9_vgui.cpp (OpStatus, NoticeClock, FormatClock), h9_vgui.h and
   vgui_H9Panel.cpp.

15. The scoreboard's SCORE and kills columns (H9Kills)

   While a server's frags are points (H9's ts_matchmode with tsm_score 1: each player's match points
   are his frags, and each side's are its TeamScore), the scoreboard heads the frags column SCORE
   and adds a K column beside it with the real kills -- every enemy each player eliminated on the
   map -- from the H9Kills user message (byte slot, short kills; slot 0 only says the frags are
   points), hooked in hud.cpp and kept in extra_player_info_t's kills, with each team's header
   summing its players'. The first H9Kills of a level turns it on and the level change turns it off;
   on any other server the K column has no width and the frags column reads K as before.
   vgui_ScorePanel.cpp's H9 KILLS blocks, SB_CELL_KCOUNT in vgui_ScorePanel.h.

16. The PPSh-41, weapon 39, and a 14th ammo caliber

   cl_dll\ev_ts.cpp (the fire sound table: h9\ppsh41\fire.wav, no silenced one), ts_buymenu.cpp (a
   row in the SMG category, PPSh-41 at 2000 and slot 35; caliber 14's pool of 71 rounds a buy at 150
   and 213 carried; the restriction bound is the weapon count), ts_weaponbar.cpp and ts_weaponbar.h (select-bar
   column 3, row 7, icon sprites\weapons\ppsh41.spr; the bar's own count of 38 weapons, which hid weapon
   38 and up, is the roster's). TS 3.0's weapons are 0 to 37 and 38 is empty, so every table has the
   PPSh-41 at 39. The weapon's table row is the shared ts_weapon_info.cpp, the same as the server's
   (mp.dll.patch, change 9). The client.dll this replaced is kept as client.dll.bak_preppsh41 in
   reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

17. Everything else reTS already had

   The reTS client is a recompilation of the whole TS client, so it also carries the third-person
   self-transparency fade, the predicted viewmodel body, the HUD and the rest of the reTS client
   work -- none of that is a patch on top of the stock DLL.

Building and deploying
----------------------

    cd reTS
    $env:RETS_REBUILD = '1'
    .\scripts\build-win32-dropin.ps1

writes binaries\ts\cl_dlls\client.dll (and the server's mp.dll). xash.bat's assemble step sets
RETS_REBUILD=1, so the H9XASH package takes this pair. The GoldSrc package does not yet: while
reTS\binaries\goldsrc\ holds a pair, export.bat's GoldSrc block copies that one into export\ts\,
the last pair built for 32 player slots (client.dll 895488 bytes, SHA256
92E1BFF2614EA881692CDEA86BAF405AD95D3F70BDBDE19BD958C8E0A5267E97), and the Steam install keeps it.
This pair goes there once it has passed the regression tests at 32 (docs\PLAYER_LIMIT_128.txt,
section 23) and reTS\binaries\goldsrc\ is removed.

The older stock client with the binary cloak patch (865280 bytes, MD5
74fc8b3039032a31f7d81440bbde0e80) is kept in
backups\goldsrc_source_build_20261029\cl_dlls\client.dll and decompile_dll\client_patched_cloak.dll;
the appendix describes that build.

